On Wed, 17 Jun 2026, Matt Jacobson wrote:
> The Mac Quadra 800 contains a "SuperDrive" floppy drive that supports
> both GCR (400 kB and 800 kB) and MFM (1440 kB) diskettes.  Implement
> an emulated version, to be controlled by a forthcoming SWIM2 device.

A bit more explanation could be useful here. In the cover letter it's 
mentioned that GCR/MFM encoding is generated by this emulation. Maybe that 
should be mentioned here. But I don't see what it's needed for. I thought 
maybe for single bit reads but those are not emulated. So is encoded data 
needed at all or could be added later when emulating single bit reads 
making this a simpler patch?

My original question that lead to to above was if the asserts in GCR 
encoding and elsewhere could be caused by the user supplying invalid data 
(i.e. bad disk image file) and thus crashing QEMU but I think if the disk 
images contain decoded data not raw GCR/MFM encoded images then these 
asserts would only be caused by errors in encoding functions in this file 
but I'm not completely sure.

